GBA hardware feature usage: Interrupts / DMA / BIOS (SWI)
=========================================================

Scope: what the DiscoBSD GBA port CURRENTLY uses of the GBA's
interrupt, DMA, and BIOS-call hardware. File and function references point at
the authoritative code; this file is a map, not a spec.


1. INTERRUPTS
-------------

Enabled hardware interrupt sources (REG_IE = IRQ_TIMER0):

    bit3  Timer0 overflow   system clock -> hardclock()   (IRQ_TIMER0)
                            clock.c:cpu_initclocks(), machdep.c:irq_enable()

Timer0 is the ONLY enabled source. VBLANK (bit0, IRQ_VBLANK) was
formerly enabled too (DISPSTAT bit3 + REG_IE bit0) but its handler only
set vblank_flag, which nothing read, so it was removed -- on-screen
input and the cursor blink are polled via REG_VCOUNT/lbolt, not a VBLANK
IRQ. Keypad, SIO (serial), DMA-completion, HBlank and VCount interrupts
are all UNUSED -- input and video timing are polled (swkbd.c reads
REG_KEYINPUT and REG_VCOUNT directly).  Exception: SERIAL_CONSOLE builds
(GBAED) also enable the SIO interrupt (bit7) as a backstop - in UART mode
it fires on send-full, receive-EMPTY or error, never on a byte arriving,
so the receive FIFO is still emptied by polling (gba_sio_uart.c).

(GBA REG_IE bit numbering: bit0=VBLANK, bit1=HBlank, bit2=VCount,
bit3=Timer0, bit12=Keypad -- see gba.h IRQ_* defines.)

Control registers:
    REG_IME       0x4000208   master enable
    REG_IE        0x4000200   per-source enable
    REG_IF        0x4000202   pending flags (write-1-to-clear)
    REG_DISPSTAT  0x4000004   bit3 = VBLANK IRQ enable

Timer0 config (clock.c):
    REG_TM0CNT_L = 65536 - (16777216 / 1024 / HZ)   ; HZ=100 -> 65372
    REG_TM0CNT_H = 0x00C3   ; 0x03=/1024 prescale, 0x40=IRQ, 0x80=start
    16.78 MHz / 1024 -> 100 Hz, drives hardclock().

Timer2 + Timer3 (clock.c), no IRQ: Timer2 runs like Timer0 (/1024, same
reload) and Timer3 counts its overflows (count-up), i.e. one per tick in
real time even while IME=0.  The Timer0 ISR replays missed hardclock()s
from Timer3's delta; swkbd.c and gba_audio.c read it too.
    GOTCHA: Timer2 is NOT a free-running 16-bit counter.  With the
    hardclock reload it counts 65373..65535 and wraps every 10 ms, so
    a plain (uint16_t)(now - then) of TM2CNT_L jumps by ~65000 at a
    wrap.  For finer-than-tick timing use Timer3 * 163 + (Timer2 -
    65373) - gba_sio_uart.c gsio_now()/gsio_since().  Getting this
    wrong once made /dev/sio end every burst read within 10 ms and
    look as if 115200 were too fast for the UART.

/dev/sio burst reads (gba_sio_uart.c sioread()) keep IME=0 until the
line goes quiet or the read is full: up to ~140 ms at 115200 for a
1600-byte read.  Ticks are caught up afterwards, but swkbd/console
polling waits and PCM playback would skip meanwhile.

Dispatch path:
    1. GBA BIOS reads the user IRQ vector at 0x03007FFC and jumps there.
       locore0.S installs gba_intr_handler into that slot.
    2. gba_intr_handler (ARM, IWRAM): IME=0; save r0-r12,lr; switch
       IRQ->SYS mode to also save the *interrupted* code's live lr
       (IRQ-mode lr is banked and lost across the mode switch); call
       gba_do_schedule(); IME=1; bx lr (returns via BIOS).
    3. gba_do_schedule() (C, Thumb, IWRAM): read REG_IF; TIMER0 ->
       myticks++, hardclock() and the rest; write REG_IF to ACK.
       machdep.c:gba_do_schedule()
    4. Preemption: if user code was interrupted and a process switch or
       a signal is due (gba_irq_preempt(), machdep.c), the handler
       pushes the interrupted pc and CPSR on the user stack and returns
       into gba_preempt_tramp (locore.S) instead. That saves all
       registers, makes a getpid syscall - whose exit runs userret(),
       the only place the kernel switches processes or delivers signals
       - then restores everything via an SVC-mode movs pc, lr.

IME discipline (the load-bearing invariant of this port):
  - The syscall trampoline holds IME=0 for the ENTIRE syscall_handler
    run: it uses r0-r2 as live scratch across raw instructions with no
    reentrancy protection (GDB-confirmed: enabling IME mid-trampoline
    let an IRQ clobber r0-r2 and crash the next syscall).
    machdep.c (gba_irq_allow/gba_irq_block comment), syscall.c
  - Only idle() is bracketed by gba_irq_allow()/gba_irq_block()
    (IME=1/0), so Timer0 can fire while idle to expire tsleep timeouts
    (select() etc.) via hardclock/softclock, then interrupts are
    re-masked before resuming a process.  machdep.c:idle()
  - locore0.S forces IME=0 at the very top of reset: a reboot is a warm
    jump to the ROM entry (see 3. below), which does not reset I/O
    space, so IME must not be assumed to start at 0.

Note: the installed handler is the asm wrapper gba_intr_handler (wired
to 0x03007FFC); a bare compiled-C handler is deliberately NOT used, as
the BIOS trampoline saves no registers before the jump -- see the
locore0.S comment at the vector install for why.


2. DMA
------

    DMA0  unused
    DMA1  sound FIFO A (PCM, left / mono)   gba_audio.c
    DMA2  sound FIFO B (PCM, right)         gba_audio.c
    DMA3  general purpose:
            EverDrive SD block transfers    ed.c
            LCD console scroll and clear    gba_text.c:gtxt_dma16()

DMA1/2 run in sound-FIFO mode (32-bit, repeat, start on FIFO request),
clocked by Timer1 (the sample clock).  A sound DMA and the SD DMA must
not share the bus: on real GBAED hardware that wedged the machine, so
ed.c pauses the sample clock (audio_suspend()/audio_resume()) around its
SD DMA, and md_gba.c does the same around long PSRAM swap copies.

DMA3 registers:
    DMA_SRC 0x040000D4  DMA_DST 0x040000D8
    DMA_LEN 0x040000DC  DMA_CTR 0x040000DE

SD transfers are 16-bit (32-bit bit 0x0400 not set); LEN=256 halfwords
= 512 bytes = one SD sector.

    ed_sd_dma_wr        mem     -> SD_DAT   CTR 0x8040  enable + dest fixed
    ed_sd_read_crc_ram  SD_RAM  -> mem      CTR 0x8100  CRC readback
    ed_sd_dma_to_rom    ROM win -> buf      CTR 0x8000  (16-bit DMA can't
                                                        write ROM directly,
                                                        so route via AUTO_WE)
    ed_sd_dma_rd        SD_RAM  -> mem      CTR 0x8000  (ROM-space dst goes
                                                        via ed_sd_dma_to_rom)

The console uses 32-bit DMA3 transfers: a copy to scroll the text up,
and a fixed-source fill (from a static zero word) to clear rows.

Completion is POLLED, for two INDEPENDENT reasons -- keep them separate:

  (A) Why no DMA-completion interrupt (a design choice, NOT hang-driven):
      the transfers are short and run in synchronous paths, so an IRQ +
      sleep/wakeup would be pure overhead. Moreover these paths run with
      IME=0 (syscall region), so a completion IRQ could not fire there.
      (The CPU is stopped while DMA3 runs anyway.)

  (B) Why the SD poll loop is bounded (ed_dma_wait: DMA_CTR busy bit
      0x8000, max 50000 iters): infinite-loop protection, grounded in
      real-hardware hangs of sibling ED_STAT_SD_BUSY waits in ed.c under
      fsck's scattered access (a wedged SD).  Three of the four SD DMA
      sites read from the SD data register, so a stuck SD can stall the
      DMA itself - the same root cause.


3. BIOS / SWI
-------------

No BIOS SWI is issued by the GBA and GBAED kernels.

  - Reboot (cpu_reboot(), machdep.c) masks interrupts, stops DMA,
    timers and sound, blanks the display, clears EWRAM, and jumps to
    the ROM entry 0x08000000; locore0.S then reinitialises everything
    as on a cold boot.  The BIOS resets were dropped: HardReset (swi
    0x26, undocumented) hangs mGBA, and SoftReset (swi 0x00) left stale
    I/O state that made the next boot misbehave.
  - Halt (machdep.c) polls REG_KEYINPUT (and, on GBAED, the serial
    line) for a key to reboot.
  - System calls do NOT use a real SWI. The GBA SWI vector 0x00000018
    is BIOS ROM (unwritable), so this port uses a software-simulated
    SWI: RAM address SYSCALL_VECTOR_ADDR = 0x03007E00 (machparam.h,
    SYSVEC in the kernel linker scripts) holds a pointer to the kernel's
    simulate_swi_via_inline_data() trampoline, written once at boot
    (machdep.c:startup()); userland calls through that pointer.
    The CONCEPT of this simulated SWI (calling the kernel through a
    fixed RAM vector, since the real SWI vector is BIOS ROM) is inspired
    by the Prex Game Boy Advance port's approach; the implementation is
    this port's own (no Prex code is used).
  - Power-saving BIOS calls (Halt swi 0x02, Stop, IntrWait,
    VBlankIntrWait) are unused. idle() busy-waits with IME toggling.

Exception: the GBALOG kernel's AGBPrint output (dev/mgba_uart.c) issues
swi 0xFA to flush its buffer; mGBA and VisualBoyAdvance intercept it.

BIOS mechanisms relied upon: the boot (header check, then a jump to
0x08000000) and the IRQ dispatch (reads the user vector at 0x03007FFC,
and "bx lr" returns through the BIOS).


Display power (roadmap #6, DONE):
  Console screen blanking is implemented as a plain register write, not a
  BIOS SWI. After console_blank_secs idle seconds (SCREEN_BLANK_SECS
  option, default 60; runtime-tunable via the machdep.console_blank
  sysctl) idle() calls gtxt_blank(1), which clears BG2 in REG_DISPCNT so
  only the (black) backdrop shows; any console output or input calls
  console_activity() to restore it. VRAM is untouched, so text returns
  intact. The GBA's backlight is not software-controllable, so this stops
  BG/VRAM fetch and darkens the panel rather than cutting the backlight.
  gba_text.c: gtxt_blank(); gba/machdep.c: console_activity()/idle().

FOLLOW-UP OPPORTUNITIES (not yet done)
--------------------------------------
  * idle() busy-waits; BIOS Halt (swi 0x02) would additionally cut CPU
    power/heat on real hardware (a complement to the display blanking of
    #6). Needs a wake source -- likely keypad IRQ -- and would need
    checking in mGBA as well as on real hardware.
  * Keypad IRQ unused (polling suffices today; needed if Halt is added).
